"smiles": "O=C1CC[C@H](C(=O)N[C@H](CCCCCS)C(=O)Nc2ccc(C(=O)O)cc2)N1",
"modification": "Introduction of a carboxylic acid on the phenyl ring",
"rationale": "HDAC1 possesses a zinc-binding domain that utilizes a conserved zinc ion to coordinate catalytic residues. Introducing a carboxylic acid group on the phenyl ring provides an additional, potentially strong, coordinating group for the zinc ion. This could enhance binding affinity and improve potency. The position *ortho* to the amide linkage is chosen to minimize steric clashes and maximize the potential for favorable interactions with the binding pocket.  The carboxylic acid is also a common feature in many HDAC inhibitors, suggesting it's a generally beneficial motif.  Synthetically, this is achievable via directed ortho-metallation followed by carboxylation, or through a Suzuki coupling with a boronic acid derivative."
    		 	 	 	 	 	 	 	,"expected_fold_improvement": 1.5
    		 	 	 	 	 	 	 	,"confidence": "medium"
},
{
"smiles": "O=C1CC[C@H](C(=O)N[C@H](CCCCCS)C(=O)Nc2ccc(Cl)cc2)N1",
"modification": "Introduction of a chlorine atom on the phenyl ring",
"rationale": "Halogen bonding is an increasingly recognized interaction in drug design. Introducing a chlorine atom on the phenyl ring can create a halogen bond with a suitable acceptor in the HDAC1 binding pocket. This interaction, while relatively weak individually, can contribute significantly to overall binding affinity when strategically positioned. The *meta* position is selected to avoid steric clashes with the amide and to allow for a more linear halogen bond.  Chlorine is also a relatively small halogen, minimizing disruption to the overall shape of the molecule.  This modification is synthetically accessible via electrophilic aromatic substitution."
    		 	 	 	 	 	 	 	,"expected_fold_improvement": 1.2
    		 	 	 	 	 	 	 	,"confidence": "low"
},
{
"smiles": "O=C1CC[C@H](C(=O)N[C@H](CCCCCS)C(=O)Nc2ccc(N)cc2)N1",
"modification": "Introduction of an amine group on the phenyl ring",
"rationale": "The introduction of an amine group on the phenyl ring could provide a hydrogen bond donor/acceptor interaction with the HDAC1 binding pocket. This could improve binding affinity and selectivity. The amine group can also be protonated at physiological pH, creating a positive charge that could interact with negatively charged residues in the binding site. The *para* position is chosen to maximize the potential for hydrogen bonding.  Synthetically, this could be achieved via nitration followed by reduction, or through a Buchwald-Hartwig amination."
    		 	 	 	 	 	 	 	,"expected_fold_improvement": 1.3
    		 	 	 	 	 	 	 	,"confidence": "medium"
},
{
"smiles": "O=C1CC[C@H](C(=O)N[C@H](CCCCS)C(=O)Nc2ccc(C#N)cc2)N1",
"modification": "Introduction of a cyano group on the phenyl ring",
"rationale": "The cyano group is a strong dipole and can participate in dipole-dipole interactions with the target protein. It is also relatively small and rigid, minimizing conformational flexibility and potentially improving binding selectivity. The *ortho* position is selected to maximize the potential for dipole-dipole interactions.  Synthetically, this could be achieved via Sandmeyer reaction from an aniline derivative."
